    "body": "the menhir fast parse path supports complete and almost-complete terms... specifically it supports things that would be terms if a hole was inserted at the end. however, fast-parsing such almost-complete things currently loses probes.\r\n\r\neg say a `.hz`  file ends in a semicolon:\r\n\r\n```\r\nlet f = fun x -> x + 1 in\r\n^^probe(f(1));\r\n^^probe(f(2));\r\n```\r\n\r\nthis loads with both probes attached but reprints without them, so `./hazel format` and any save of a loaded-but-unedited buffer destroy the probes.\r\n\r\nCause: menhir rejects the dangling `;`, FastParse's append-a-hole retry strips the hole it added, and loading was the one path into the editor that doesn't regrout,  so the buffer ends in an operand-less `;`, `Segment.skel` fails on it, and `Triggers.refractor_seg_to_seg` gives up and reprints the program without its `^^probe(...)` wrappers (invocations nested inside tile children survive, which is why body-less let chains usually looked fine).\r\n\r\nFix: `PersistentZipper.parse_text` regrouts after unzip, same as every edit. Saving such a fragment spells the materialized hole (`...f(2));\u00bf`) and is a fixed point thereafter.\r\n\r\ncloses #2450\r\n\r\n\ud83e\udd16 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code), edited by andrew",
